        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "80e9dd9d-fa5e-5c09-8044-327774c8b6ef",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Little Bollington, Dunham Park, Worsley, Manchester, Runcorn, Leigh, Agden, Baguley, Bowdon, Dunham, Hale\n- Systems: Bridgewater Canal\n- People: Hamon de Massey, Aelfward\n- Topics: Norman conquest of England, Domesday Book, manors\n\nFacts:\n- After the Norman conquest of England in 1066, Hamon de Massey owned the manors of Agden, Baguley, Bowdon, Dunham, Hale, and Little Bollington.\n- Domesday Book states that Hamon de Massey took over those manors from the Saxon thegn Aelfward.\n- The Bridgewater Canal was opened in 1761 from Worsley to Manchester.\n- The Bridgewater Canal was later extended from Manchester to Runcorn and from Worsley to Leigh.\n- The Bridgewater Canal runs through the western side of Little Bollington.\n- Dunham Park lies to the north east of Little Bollington.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "25d5ef10-c741-5e79-bf78-7d8f45ec5ae1",
        "text": "Page: Little Bollington. Sentence: The Bridgewater Canal runs through the western side and Dunham Park lies to the north east.\nPage: Bridgewater Canal. Sentence: It was opened in 1761 from Worsley to Manchester, and later extended from Manchester to Runcorn, and then from Worsley to Leigh.\nPage: Hamon de Massey. Sentence: The first Hamon de Massey was the owner of the manors of Agden, Baguley, Bowdon, Dunham, Hale and Little Bollington after the Norman conquest of England (1066), taking over from the Saxon thegn Aelfward according to Domesday Book.",
